+Subject: [PATCH] spi: qpic-snand: use the vendor ECC strength of each flash part
+
+The Xiaomi BE7000 ships with different SPI NAND parts: most boards carry
+a GigaDevice GD5F1GQ5REYIG with 128 bytes of OOB, newer ones an ESMT
+F50D1G41LB with 64. The stock kernel picks the ECC strength per part from
+its flash table (8 bits for the GigaDevice part, 4 for the ESMT one) and
+its device tree value is not used. With the 8 bits our device tree asks
+for, every read on the ESMT part failed with -EBADMSG and UBI could not
+attach, so no build booted on those boards.
+
+Take the strength from the same table, keyed by the JEDEC ID. For parts
+it does not list, keep the device tree value unless an 8 bit codeword
+does not fit the page and its OOB, then use 4 bits.
+
+Signed-off-by: Timofey Maykov <itnitro@gmail.com>
+
+Taken from Beam WRT (GPL-2.0, author attribution kept):
+Link: https://github.com/timofey-maykov/be7000-openwrt/blob/e45121f2cb48ec9f6d74e0ed3ef9e7899fc67d4d/patches/0908-spi-qpic-snand-vendor-ecc-strength.patch
+---
+--- a/drivers/spi/spi-qpic-snand.c
++++ b/drivers/spi/spi-qpic-snand.c
+@@ -245,6 +245,50 @@
+ 	.free = qcom_spi_ooblayout_free,
+ };
+ 
++/*
++ * ECC strength the vendor firmware uses for each SPI NAND part, from the
++ * flash table of the Xiaomi BE7000 stock kernel (QSDK, Linux 5.4). Boards
++ * of one model ship with different parts, and the data the stock firmware
++ * and its bootloader write is only readable with the same strength, so pick
++ * it by the JEDEC ID rather than from the device tree.
++ */
++static const struct {
++	u8 mfr;
++	u8 dev;
++	u8 strength;
++} qcom_spi_vendor_ecc[] = {
